course: dictionary
lesson: 13546
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. The word impregnate has two main meanings. The first is to soak or fill something completely with a substance. The second, used in biology, is to make a female animal pregnant.
explain: «The fence posts were impregnated with preservative so they would last for years.» The posts were not having a baby. The clues are fence posts and preservative, so impregnated means soaked all through with a special liquid that protects the wood.
explain: «In our biology lesson, we learned that a male animal can impregnate a female so that young begin to grow inside her.» The clues are biology, male animal and young, so this is the second meaning. Always check the words around the hard word before you choose a meaning.
fact: Impregnate is a verb, and its forms are impregnate, impregnates, impregnated and impregnating.
fact: «Impregnate» can mean to soak or fill something completely with a substance.
fact: «Wood impregnated with oil» means wood that has been soaked all through with oil.
fact: In biology, «impregnate» means to make a female animal pregnant, so that a young one starts to grow inside her.
fact: Context clues, like the words fence posts, preservative or biology, tell you which meaning of a word is being used.
exercise: «The fence posts were impregnated with preservative so they would last for years.» What does impregnated mean here? | Cut into small pieces | Dug deep into the ground | *Soaked all through with a special liquid | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «In our biology lesson, we learned that a male animal can impregnate a female so that young begin to grow inside her.» What does impregnate mean in this sentence? | To run very fast | *To make a female animal pregnant | To cut open carefully | To wash clean with water
exercise: Ravi says impregnate means to paint something. Why is he wrong? | It means to dry something in the sun | It means to paint, so he is right | *It means to soak or fill something completely with a substance, not just cover its surface | It means to break something into pieces
test: «The sponge was impregnated with water until it could hold no more.» What does impregnated mean here? | Dried out in the sun | Squeezed flat on the table | *Soaked completely | Thrown away in the bin
test: Materials are ___ with chemicals to make them waterproof or fireproof. Which word fills the gap? | dropped | *impregnated | climbed | painted
exam: «The ship's sails were impregnated with a waterproof coating.» Which meaning fits best? | *Soaked or filled all through with a substance | Sailed away quickly into the sunset | Shouted loudly across the deck | Torn into long strips by the wind
